# /etc/cron.d/ianseo-nightly — nightly maintenance window (one single line).
#
# root:root, chmod 0644. ⚠️ Classic traps of /etc/cron.d:
#   - the file name may only hold [A-Za-z0-9_-]: a DOT or an extension (.cron, .conf) makes
#     it SILENTLY IGNORED;
#   - a final LINE FEED is needed, otherwise the last line is ignored;
#   - unlike "crontab -e", a USER field is needed (7 fields);
#   - the file must be neither executable nor writable by the group.
#
# This single call replaces the separate sync crons: maintenance.php runs
# maintenance ON → backup → core update → module updates → licences → logos →
# maintenance OFF, and guarantees the exit from maintenance even on failure.
#
# Started by ROOT, only to unlock the core files before, and lock them again after: the
# script itself runs as www-data (su). The web account thus never has the right to make the
# ianseo code writable — do NOT give ianseo-unlock to www-data through sudo. In
# config.local.json, maintenance.unlock and maintenance.lock therefore stay EMPTY. The ";"
# (and not "&&") before ianseo-lock guarantees the re-locking even when the maintenance fails.
#
# ⚠️ When a notice is configured (config.local.json → maintenance.notice.at), it must match
# the TIME BELOW. Neither is derived from the other: a gap would announce a maintenance at the
# wrong time.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin

15 3 * * * root /usr/local/bin/ianseo-unlock >/dev/null && su -s /bin/sh www-data -c '/usr/bin/php /var/www/ianseo/Modules/Custom/AUTH/cron/maintenance.php >> /var/log/ianseo-maintenance.log 2>&1'; /usr/local/bin/ianseo-lock >/dev/null
